---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="AI Design Repository | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I built and continue to own the shared AI design repository used across multiple Microsoft design, product, and engineering teams."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--blue">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back">← Back to selected work</a>

        <span class="tag">AI systems · Product design · Shared infrastructure</span>
        <h1>Building the repository our teams use to design with AI</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I identified a fragmented prototyping problem and built a shared AI design repository
          that became a required workflow across multiple teams. I designed the architecture,
          integrated existing capabilities, established contribution and quality controls, and
          continue to own the system as it expands from prototyping into engineering handoff.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Dozens</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Designers, product managers, and engineers across multiple teams</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Required</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Shared workflow for design and prototyping</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Active</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Used for ideation, demos, customer concepts, and handoff</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <aside class="disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
          <strong>About the evidence</strong>
          <p>This project was completed inside Microsoft. I reconstructed the diagrams with generic labels and removed product details that are not public.</p>
        </aside>

        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior Content Designer</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Ownership</dt><dd>Initiative, architecture, implementation, integrations, operating model, roadmap, and active ownership</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Collaboration</dt><dd>Design manager, designers, product managers, and engineers</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Shared design and prototyping infrastructure across multiple teams</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Active and required, with engineering handoff still evolving</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Verified internal use and generic reconstructed artifacts</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="AI design repository case study sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#problem">Workflow gap</a></li>
            <li><a href="#decision">System decision</a></li>
            <li><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></li>
            <li><a href="#operating-model">Operating model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#outcome">Outcome</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="problem" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The fragmented workflow</h2>
          <p>
            Before this repository, designers used a mix of Figma, Build, and separate prototype
            repositories to explore product ideas. Each tool could produce useful work, but the
            work remained fragmented. Designers often started from scratch, and prototypes were
            difficult to extend across the team.
          </p>
          <p>
            There was no shared operating model for repository structure, contributions, reviews,
            or quality checks. The team could create prototypes, but it could not build a
            cumulative design system around them.
          </p>
          <p>I wanted to turn isolated experiments into infrastructure the team could use together.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="ownership-title">
          <h2 id="ownership-title">Identifying and owning the opportunity</h2>
          <p>
            I began by testing the idea through a series of experiments. I identified the
            opportunity, proposed the repository, and built the system myself. I worked closely
            with the design manager to understand his requirements, needs, and vision, and we
            prioritized the roadmap together.
          </p>
          <p>
            I made most final decisions about the architecture and implementation. I also reused
            and integrated existing internal tools where they solved part of the problem well. The
            goal was not to rebuild every capability. It was to connect the right capabilities
            into one maintainable workflow.
          </p>
          <p>I continue to own the repository, its architecture, contribution process, quality controls, and roadmap.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="decision" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>One governed repository instead of disconnected tools</h2>
          <p>
            The key decision was to centralize the team's prototyping work in one governed
            repository rather than create another optional tool.
          </p>
          <p>That required decisions about more than code or folder names. I designed:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The folder and information architecture</li>
            <li>The branching model</li>
            <li>Pull-request and approval workflows</li>
            <li>How designers would contribute and update shared work</li>
            <li>How the repository would be distributed and maintained</li>
            <li>Which internal tools to integrate rather than rebuild</li>
            <li>How prototypes would move toward engineering handoff</li>
            <li>Which deterministic checks would protect quality</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The repository gave the team one shared foundation. Designers could build on each
            other's work instead of recreating it, and product managers and engineers could
            participate in the same workflow.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="workflow-caption">
          <div class="workflow-compare" role="img" aria-label="Before and after workflow comparison showing fragmented individual tools replaced by one shared governed repository">
            <section class="workflow-panel workflow-panel--before">
              <span class="diagram-label">Before</span>
              <h3>Fragmented prototypes</h3>
              <div class="tool-row" aria-hidden="true"><span>Figma</span><span>Build</span><span>Separate repos</span></div>
              <ul>
                <li>Isolated work</li>
                <li>Repeated setup</li>
                <li>Limited reuse</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
            <div class="workflow-shift" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
            <section class="workflow-panel workflow-panel--after">
              <span class="diagram-label">After</span>
              <h3>Shared repository</h3>
              <div class="tool-row" aria-hidden="true"><span>Reuse</span><span>Review</span><span>Checks</span></div>
              <ul>
                <li>Team contributions</li>
                <li>Shared foundations</li>
                <li>Demos and handoff</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="workflow-caption">
            <strong>From isolated work to shared infrastructure.</strong> One repository connected
            reusable design work, branches, reviews, checks, demos, customer concepts, and handoff.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="architecture" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Connecting the system</h2>
          <p>
            The repository does not try to contain every design or AI capability. It connects
            shared context and rules with existing MCPs, agents, knowledge bases, repositories,
            and internal tools.
          </p>
          <p>
            A request begins with a design or product goal. The repository applies shared guidance
            and uses the relevant capabilities to produce a prototype or reusable design output.
            The work then moves through review, evaluation, and handoff.
          </p>
          <p>
            I use generic terms here because the exact internal systems and implementation details
            are not public. The relationship is the important part: the repository coordinates
            specialized capabilities without duplicating them.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="architecture-caption">
          <ol class="system-flow">
            <li><span>1</span><strong>Product goal</strong><small>Design request or idea</small></li>
            <li><span>2</span><strong>Shared repository</strong><small>Context, rules, and reusable foundations</small></li>
            <li><span>3</span><strong>Connected capabilities</strong><small>MCPs, agents, knowledge bases, and tools</small></li>
            <li><span>4</span><strong>Design output</strong><small>Prototype or reusable experience</small></li>
            <li><span>5</span><strong>Review and handoff</strong><small>Evaluation, decisions, and engineering inputs</small></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="architecture-caption">
            <strong>A reconstructed system view.</strong> The repository coordinates specialized
            capabilities without exposing internal system names or implementation details.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="operating-model" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Designing the operating model</h2>
          <p>
            The repository needed to work as team infrastructure, not as a personal prototype. I
            designed an operating model for how people would use, change, and trust it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Branches and pull requests gave contributors a predictable way to propose updates.
            Approvals protected shared work. The repository's deterministic checks validate:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Evaluations</li>
            <li>Design-token compliance</li>
            <li>Content quality</li>
            <li>Accessibility</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These checks matter because generative output is not useful simply because it renders.
            It needs to follow the same standards the team expects from product design work.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="operating-caption">
          <ol class="operating-flow">
            <li><span>01</span><strong>Create a branch</strong><small>Start from the shared foundation</small></li>
            <li><span>02</span><strong>Propose a change</strong><small>Build or update reusable work</small></li>
            <li><span>03</span><strong>Run checks</strong><small>Evaluate tokens, content, and accessibility</small></li>
            <li><span>04</span><strong>Review and approve</strong><small>Protect the shared system</small></li>
            <li><span>05</span><strong>Merge and reuse</strong><small>Make approved work available to the team</small></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="operating-caption">
            <strong>A governed contribution model.</strong> Branches, checks, reviews, and approvals
            make shared design work maintainable instead of disposable.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="training-title">
          <h2 id="training-title">Preparing the team</h2>
          <p>
            The workflow also required designers to work differently. Before the wider release, I
            trained the team in VS Code, GitHub, GitHub Copilot, branches, pull requests, and
            repository-based collaboration.
          </p>
          <p>
            That training made the transition possible. The repository was not just handed to the
            team with instructions to use it. Designers had already started building the technical
            fluency they needed to contribute confidently.
          </p>
          <p>
            This team-specific work was part of a broader AI education effort across the design
            organization. I cover that organizational work separately because its scope extends
            beyond this repository.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="outcome" class="content-section">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2>What changed</h2>
            <p>
              After months of iteration, I released the mature system to dozens of designers,
              product managers, and engineers across multiple teams. It became a required workflow
              for designers and a core part of how the group develops and communicates ideas.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-grid" aria-label="Current repository uses">
            <div><strong>Design and ideation</strong><span>Shared prototypes and reusable work</span></div>
            <div><strong>Demos and customers</strong><span>Leadership reviews and customer concepts</span></div>
            <div><strong>Engineering handoff</strong><span>Components, Storybook, versions, guided runs, and backlog inputs</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="reading-column">
            <p>
              The most important result is not a speed percentage. It is that the work is
              cumulative now. Teams can extend a shared foundation instead of restarting with
              every idea.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="handoff-title">
          <h2 id="handoff-title">From prototype to engineering handoff</h2>
          <p>
            The current focus is improving how prototype decisions move into development.
            Designers and engineers are exploring how repository output can support reusable
            components, Storybook, guided runs, versions, and more structured backlog creation.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is still active work. The repository solved the fragmentation problem, but
            broader use exposed the next system-level opportunity: making the transition from
            design intent to engineering implementation more explicit and reusable.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>
            The repository changed more than the tool. It changed how designers contributed, how
            teams reused work, and how prototypes moved toward engineering.
          </p>
          <p>
            I think the biggest lesson is that adoption starts before release. The architecture,
            contribution model, quality checks, and team training all mattered because they gave
            people a reliable way to participate. Building the repository was only one part of
            building the system.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="next-system" aria-labelledby="next-system-title">
          <div>
            <span class="tag">Active system</span>
            <h2 id="next-system-title">A shared foundation for design work</h2>
            <p>The repository continues to evolve with the design, product, and engineering teams that use it. The next phase focuses on turning shared prototypes into clearer, more reusable engineering inputs.</p>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <div class="nav-row">
          <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="btn btn--outline">← All selected projects</a>
          <a href={`${base}/work/hackathon`} class="btn">Next: Hackathon PM agent →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .outcome__label { max-width: 25ch; font-size: var(--size-sm); color: var(--muted); }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .content-section ul { margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6); }
  .content-section li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }

  .disclosure { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: 0 auto var(--space-12); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; }
  .disclosure strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .disclosure p { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }

  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; line-height: 1.2; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc li { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-coral); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .case-toc li + li { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .case-toc li + li::before { content: ''; width: 2px; height: 16px; margin-right: var(--space-4); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--toc-divider); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n + 1) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.3; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }

  .evidence-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption { width: 100%; margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }

  .workflow-compare { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-3); align-items: stretch; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 20px; background: #fff; }
  .workflow-panel { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .workflow-panel--before { background: var(--portfolio-cream); }
  .workflow-panel:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .workflow-panel h3 { margin-block: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); color: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .workflow-panel ul { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .workflow-panel li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .diagram-label { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .tool-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .tool-row span { padding: 0.3rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 999px; background: #fff; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; }
  .workflow-shift { display: grid; place-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; }

  .system-flow, .operating-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); list-style: none; }
  .system-flow li, .operating-flow li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 185px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .system-flow li:hover, .operating-flow li:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .system-flow li::after, .operating-flow li::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -0.8rem; z-index: 1; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .system-flow li:last-child::after, .operating-flow li:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .system-flow li > span, .operating-flow li > span { display: grid; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .system-flow strong, .operating-flow strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .system-flow small, .operating-flow small { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .operating-flow { border-color: #e1c35f; background: #fff8da; }

  .impact-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto; }
  .impact-grid div { display: flex; min-height: 150px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .impact-grid div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .impact-grid strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); line-height: 1.35; }
  .impact-grid span { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.5; }

  .next-system { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .next-system .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .next-system h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .next-system p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }
  .nav-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .divider { margin-block: var(--space-16) var(--space-8); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .case-toc ul { gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; }
    .case-toc li + li { margin-left: 0; }
    .case-toc li + li::before { content: none; }
    .system-flow, .operating-flow { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .system-flow li, .operating-flow li { min-height: 150px; }
    .system-flow li::after, .operating-flow li::after { content: none; }
    .impact-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .impact-grid div { min-height: 0; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 130px; }
    .disclosure { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-meta { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc { margin-top: var(--space-6); margin-bottom: var(--space-12); padding-block: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6); }
    .case-toc a { min-height: 44px; }
    .workflow-compare { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .workflow-shift { transform: rotate(90deg); }
    .system-flow, .operating-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .system-flow li, .operating-flow li { min-height: auto; }
    .nav-row .btn { width: 100%; text-align: center; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .outcome:hover,
    .case-meta div:hover,
    .workflow-panel:hover,
    .system-flow li:hover,
    .operating-flow li:hover,
    .impact-grid div:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
